WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.916
Aesop’s Fables. Part 6.

00:00:02.916 --> 00:00:04.833
The Fox and the Stork.

00:00:04.833 --> 00:00:11.669
A Fox invited a Stork to dinner, at which the only fare provided was a large flat dish of soup.

00:00:11.669 --> 00:00:19.624
The Fox lapped it up with great relish, but the Stork with her long bill tried in vain to partake of the savoury broth.

00:00:19.624 --> 00:00:23.893
Her evident distress caused the sly Fox much amusement.

00:00:23.893 --> 00:00:33.228
But not long after the Stork invited him in turn, and set before him a pitcher with a long and narrow neck, into which she could get her bill with ease.

00:00:33.228 --> 00:00:43.173
Thus, while she enjoyed her dinner, the Fox sat by hungry and helpless, for it was impossible for him to reach the tempting contents of the vessel.

00:00:43.173 --> 00:00:45.438
The Wolf in Sheep’s Clothing.

00:00:45.438 --> 00:00:52.584
A Wolf resolved to disguise himself in order that he might prey upon a flock of sheep without fear of detection.

00:00:52.584 --> 00:00:58.943
So he clothed himself in a sheepskin, and slipped among the sheep when they were out at pasture.

00:00:58.943 --> 00:01:05.512
He completely deceived the shepherd, and when the flock was penned for the night he was shut in with the rest.

00:01:05.512 --> 00:01:17.284
But that very night as it happened, the shepherd, requiring a supply of mutton for the table, laid hands on the Wolf in mistake for a Sheep, and killed him with his knife on the spot.

00:01:17.284 --> 00:01:19.437
The Stag in the Ox-Stall.

00:01:19.437 --> 00:01:36.471
A Stag, chased from his lair by the hounds, took refuge in a farmyard, and, entering a stable where a number of oxen were stalled, thrust himself under a pile of hay in a vacant stall, where he lay concealed, all but the tips of his horns.

00:01:36.471 --> 00:01:41.703
Presently one of the Oxen said to him, "What has induced you to come in here?

00:01:41.703 --> 00:01:46.123
Aren't you aware of the risk you are running of being captured by the herdsmen?"

00:01:46.123 --> 00:01:50.321
To which he replied, "Pray let me stay for the present.

00:01:50.321 --> 00:01:54.580
When night comes I shall easily escape under cover of the dark."

00:01:54.580 --> 00:02:10.711
In the course of the afternoon more than one of the farm-hands came in, to attend to the wants of the cattle, but not one of them noticed the presence of the Stag, who accordingly began to congratulate himself on his escape and to express his gratitude to the Oxen.

00:02:10.711 --> 00:02:16.908
"We wish you well," said the one who had spoken before, "but you are not out of danger yet.

00:02:16.908 --> 00:02:23.658
If the master comes, you will certainly be found out, for nothing ever escapes his keen eyes."

00:02:23.658 --> 00:02:30.413
Presently, sure enough, in he came, and made a great to-do about the way the Oxen were kept.

00:02:30.413 --> 00:02:37.904
"The beasts are starving," he cried; "here, give them more hay, and put plenty of litter under them."

00:02:37.904 --> 00:02:45.854
As he spoke, he seized an armful himself from the pile where the Stag lay concealed, and at once detected him.

00:02:45.854 --> 00:02:50.787
Calling his men, he had him seized at once and killed for the table.

00:02:50.787 --> 00:02:52.783
The Milkmaid and her Pail.

00:02:52.783 --> 00:03:00.260
A farmer's daughter had been out to milk the cows, and was returning to the dairy carrying her pail of milk upon her head.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:11.192
As she walked along, she fell a-musing after this fashion: "The milk in this pail will provide me with cream, which I will make into butter and take to market to sell.

00:03:11.192 --> 00:03:20.645
With the money I will buy a number of eggs, and these, when hatched, will produce chickens, and by and by I shall have quite a large poultry-yard.

00:03:20.645 --> 00:03:37.598
Then I shall sell some of my fowls, and with the money which they will bring in I will buy myself a new gown, which I shall wear when I go to the fair; and all the young fellows will admire it, and come and make love to me, but I shall toss my head and have nothing to say to them."

00:03:37.598 --> 00:03:43.652
Forgetting all about the pail, and suiting the action to the word, she tossed her head.

00:03:43.652 --> 00:03:50.889
Down went the pail, all the milk was spilled, and all her fine castles in the air vanished in a moment!

00:03:50.889 --> 00:03:53.916
Do not count your chickens before they are hatched.

00:03:54.531 --> 00:03:57.715
The Dolphins, the Whales, and the Sprat.

00:03:57.715 --> 00:04:03.825
The Dolphins quarrelled with the Whales, and before very long they began fighting with one another.

00:04:03.825 --> 00:04:17.805
The battle was very fierce, and had lasted some time without any sign of coming to an end, when a Sprat thought that perhaps he could stop it; so he stepped in and tried to persuade them to give up fighting and make friends.

00:04:17.805 --> 00:04:26.520
But one of the Dolphins said to him contemptuously, "We would rather go on fighting till we're all killed than be reconciled by a Sprat like you!"
